  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Solve for $n$ where $\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{n}.$\n\n$n=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $n$ and ${n}^{2}.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(n,{n}^{2})={n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by ${n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}(\\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}}) & ={n}^{2}(\\frac{6}{n}) \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & ={n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ {n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{{n}^{2}}+{n}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{{n}^{2}} & =n\\cdot n\\cdot \\frac{6}{n} \\\\ \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n+7}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}+\\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{n-5}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}} & =n\\cdot \\cancel{n}\\cdot \\frac{6}{\\cancel{n}} \\\\ n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n+7+n-5 & =n\\cdot 6 \\\\ 2n+2 & =6n \\\\ -4n & =-2 \\\\ n & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
